WEBVTT - PauseAI Educational Transcript

00:00:00.000 --> 00:01:00.000
Welcome to Machine Learning Fundamentals. In this lecture, we explore neural network architectures, optimization dynamics, and modern training paradigms.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:02:15.000
An artificial neuron models basic decision boundaries. It computes an affine transformation: z equals the dot product of weights W and input vector X plus bias b.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:03:30.000
To evaluate how accurately our neural network predicts targets, we define an objective loss function. Mean Squared Error measures quadratic distance for regression, while Cross-Entropy evaluates probability distributions in classification.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:04:45.000
Gradient Descent is our primary optimization engine. By computing the gradient vector of the loss with respect to parameters, we update weights in the direction of steepest descent scaled by our learning rate eta.

00:04:45.000 --> 00:06:00.000
Activation functions introduce non-linearity. Without non-linearities, any deep stack of linear layers collapses mathematically into a single linear matrix multiplication. The Sigmoid function compresses values between 0 and 1, but saturates. ReLU outputs max of 0 and x, overcoming vanishing gradients.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:07:30.000
Now let us tackle Backpropagation. Backpropagation is the foundational algorithm that enables multi-layer neural network training through recursive application of the calculus chain rule.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:09:00.000
During the backward pass of backpropagation, the error gradient flows in reverse from the output layer back toward earlier hidden layers, computing partial derivatives dL over dW efficiently.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:10:30.000
When neural networks have high capacity, they risk overfitting to training noise. We mitigate overfitting through regularization strategies like L2 weight decay and Dropout, which randomly disables neurons during forward passes.
